    "A person's persistence at a dull task recovers only after rest, however the task is framed or rewarded.",
    [("Someone who has worked hard on a report all afternoon feels a growing pull towards a film she has been looking forward to.",
      "Paragraph 3 says weariness nudges attention 'towards rest, pleasure or novelty' as the value of alternatives accumulates, which this describes."),
     ("A volunteer stays absorbed in demanding work for longer when he can see clearly how it helps the people he serves.",
      "Paragraph 4 says tasks that 'feel meaningful or rewarding should be less draining', so this is just what the account predicts."),
     ("Two equally demanding tasks leave people differently tired, depending on how rewarding each of the tasks feels to them.",
      "Paragraph 4 contrasts tasks that feel rewarding with 'equally demanding tasks that do not', which is this case exactly.")],
    "Paragraph 4 says that 'changing how a task is framed, or what it leads to, may restore persistence where rest alone would not'. A case in which only rest helps, whatever the framing or reward, is what the fuel picture would predict and runs against the opportunity cost account.",
    "Treating the film case as inconsistent because it describes giving up, though the account explains giving up as a shift in priorities.",
    "Three options are close paraphrases of predictions in paragraphs 3 and 4; the key echoes the rival model and must be recognised as such.",
